annotations:
  - {"kind":"user","id":"81c554e4-2f3d-4675-86a1-53009b5357ce","permanentBaseCommit":"b4e4ab3745097b6c6b9c5f81915678d70beb4b4a","message":"How do we spawn new worktrees in the current implementation?  confirm","preset":"%Q","scope":"line","anchor":{"line":20,"text":"","before":["- `sundial` CLI owns specs, decisions, research, managed spec-worktree topology, creation, finishing, and Git safety.","- `sundial-editor-cli` owns named agents, sessions, queues, and agent work.","- The Sundial VS Code extension owns card presentation, user prompts, progress, capability checks, and `vscode.openFolder` navigation. It does not reproduce either CLI's mutations."],"after":["The installed context verified on 2026-07-23 is:","- `arcridge.sundial` 0.4.2 and `@arcridge/sundial` 0.4.0;","- `arcridge.sundial-editor` 0.16.0 and `@arcridge/sundial-editor-cli` 0.9.0;"]},"officialResponses":[{"userAnnotationId":"81c554e4-2f3d-4675-86a1-53009b5357ce","agentId":"8f34a90c-0839-4ed2-9d7d-6b9bf75b7058","agentSessionId":"d7d661fd-925d-4c20-a0d7-97cb29c116e9","body":"## Confirmed current behavior\n\nIn the shipped VS Code extension (`arcridge.sundial` **0.4.2**), new worktrees are spawned directly by the extension host—not by `sundial-editor-cli`.\n\n1. The user invokes the fork action from the Specs sidebar/board or runs `sundial.specs.spawnWorktree` (`packages/vscode/src/extension.ts:346-347`, `400-401`, `547`).\n2. The host resolves the selected spec file and workspace root, then calls `spawnSpecWorktree` (`extension.ts:1253-1268`).\n3. It refuses immediately if the current workspace is already a linked Git worktree (`packages/vscode/src/specWorktrees.ts:113-115`). The UI also disables the action in that state (`extension.ts:959-960`, `984-985`, `1061-1062`).\n4. It derives the default branch and directory name from the spec filename without `.md`, prompts for the branch, and validates it with `git check-ref-format --branch` (`specWorktrees.ts:111`, `128-141`, `195-205`).\n5. It inspects `git worktree list --porcelain`. If that branch is already checked out, it offers to open the existing worktree rather than creating another (`specWorktrees.ts:144-163`).\n6. Otherwise, the default path is a **sibling of the repository**, not inside it: `path.join(path.dirname(gitRoot), specFileBasename)` (`specWorktrees.ts:204-206`). For `/repos/codesteward` and this spec, that would be `/repos/SPEC-0011-expand-worktree-support`.\n7. It runs one of:\n   - `git worktree add -b <branch> <siblingPath> HEAD` for a new branch; or\n   - `git worktree add <siblingPath> <branch>` for an existing local branch (`specWorktrees.ts:175-183`).\n8. It opens the resulting folder with `vscode.openFolder`, a file URI, and `{ forceNewWindow: true }` (`extension.ts:1282-1284`).\n\nTherefore, the `.sundial-worktrees/<spec-file-basename>` location, `.git/info/exclude` update, creation from linked worktrees, editor-CLI ownership, and current-window reuse described in SPEC-0011 are **planned behavior, not the current 0.4.2 implementation**. Current code uses a sibling directory, blocks creation from linked worktrees, performs Git mutations inside the VS Code extension, makes no local-exclude change, and opens a new window.\n\n## Validation\n\nRead-only source inspection was supplemented by the existing compiled focused unit suites:\n\n`node --test packages/vscode/out/unit/specWorktrees.test.js packages/vscode/out/unit/packageManifest.test.js`\n\nResult: **21/21 tests passed**. No product files were changed.\n","createdAt":"2026-07-23T16:27:11.176Z"}],"agentAnnotations":[]}
